# What the original formation readout measures

Personal conditional derivation, 2026-09-24. Independent PRE pending.
The target is an operational characterization of the already supplied
instrument. No new detector, positive-frequency coupling, physical parameter
selection, empirical fit or framework-wide impossibility is assumed.

## 1. Fixed domain and exact initial instrument

Use the full compensated rotor matter–field law on a finite simple cubic
torus, all periods even and at least six, with the specified all-A-plus and
B-empty initial matter. Keep the original resolved formation labels. For
each plaquette p choose one ordered pair j_p,l_p in the established readout
geometry by a cubic isometry. The two opposite A corners and the outward
birth sites are retained. The exact initial-sector effects are

    B_j*B_j=5 I,
    O_p=(B_l B_j)*B_l B_j=23 I+W_p+W_p*,
    W_p unitary.                                                (1)

These are the pinned readout result, not newly derived path algebra here.
The coherent-sign multiplicity is valid for the boundary effect only.
Every later-time expression must use the actual chosen marks and the full
matter-dependent electric and magnetic generators. Equation (1) is not a
full-space identity after arbitrary matter formation.

For a normalized physical initial field rho, the initial marked intensities
are R_j(0)=R_l(0)=5 kappa. The zero-positive-lag factorial density is
F_jl(0,0+)=kappa^2 Tr(O_p rho). Consequently the particular dimensionless
normalized mark statistic is exactly

    G_record(rho) = F_jl(0,0+) / [R_j(0) R_l(0)]
                  = [23+2 Re Tr(W_p rho)]/25,
                  21/25 <= G_record <= 1.                       (2)

It is an initial boundary statistic, not a stationary optical autocorrelation
or a finite-bin theorem. No assertion that the endpoints are attained by
normalizable chart packets is needed for the bounds. Finite-width integrals
converge to it at fixed parameters by trace-class continuity. There is no
uniform-in-energy lag modulus in this observation.

Every first-event label and its first-event time has a state-independent
distribution in this initial matter sector: the total rate is 30 kappa V,
and each resolved label has rate 5 kappa. Any classical random relabeling
of that first label/time, independent of the unknown state, also has a
state-independent distribution. This statement ends at the first-event
record; it excludes neither later histories nor a differently prepared
matter detector. It does not say that the first event has no backaction.

## 2. Leading response for finite-Hermite preparations

Use the fixed-volume weak-field map of the pinned packet theorem. Write
tau_*=a/c, K=g^2/(2tau_*), delta=1/(4tau_*g^2). On the finite transverse
real space let D_curl f_r=lambda_r f_r, with real orthonormal f_r and
lambda_r>0. Put omega_r=sqrt(lambda_r)/tau_* and

    d_pr=(c_p.f_r)/sqrt(2 sqrt(lambda_r)),
    X_p(s)=sum_r d_pr [a_r exp(-i omega_r s)+a_r* exp(i omega_r s)],
    v_p=sum_r d_pr^2.

The state psi is any fixed normalized finite linear combination of harmonic
occupation states. Its reference orbit is exp(-i H_h s)psi, and its actual
initial-sector orbit is the normalized compact rotor preparation evolved
by the actual H_g. The reference characteristic function is that of
exp(i g X_p(s)). Define

    N_p(s)=sum_rt d_pr d_pt exp(i(omega_r-omega_t)s)
                                      <psi|a_r* a_t|psi>,
    A_p(s)=sum_rt d_pr d_pt exp(-i(omega_r+omega_t)s)
                                      <psi|a_r a_t|psi>.

N_p is nonnegative: it is the expectation of A* A for
A=sum_r d_pr exp(-i omega_r s)a_r. Direct expansion with the canonical
commutators gives

    <X_p(s)^2>_psi-v_p=2N_p(s)+2 Re A_p(s).                     (3)

Subtract the same-vacuum response, and define the deficit of the prepared
coincidence effect relative to its vacuum reference by

    D_p(g,s)=<O_p>_actual,vac(s)-<O_p>_actual,psi(s).

Then, uniformly on each fixed finite observation interval,

    D_p(g,s)=2g^2[N_p(s)+Re A_p(s)]+O_T(g^4).                  (4)

Proof of the remainder: 2(cos(gX)-1)+g^2 X^2 has expectation bounded by
g^4 <X^4>/12. Finite Hermite superpositions have uniform finite moments
on every fixed-time interval. The quotient cutoff and normalization have
exponentially small tails. The parent supplies actual/reference vector
error O_T(g^2). For O_p-25I=W_p+W_p*-2I its action on the reference
vector has norm O(g^2). Expanding its expectation gives a cross term and
an error square of order g^4, using exact normalization to remove the
identity. No unbounded moment is applied to an uncontrolled vector error.

This argument does not require psi to have definite parity. In contrast,
extensions of the separate centered magnetic-lag estimate to a general
finite-Hermite state require its vanishing first coordinate moment, or a
new bound for the linear term. We do not silently import a finite-lag
rate for arbitrary displaced or parity-mixed states here.

For any state supported within a fixed total-number sector, A_p=0.
Its leading response is the nonnegative positive-frequency quadratic
N_p. It can have spatial interference through off-diagonal occupation
coherences. This includes the earlier moving one-particle packet.

For n quanta in one fixed real mode r with all other modes vacant, the
harmonic reference has the exact characteristic function

    <W_p>_n=exp(-g^2 v_p/2) L_n(g^2 d_pr^2),                  (5)

where L_n is the ordinary Laguerre polynomial. This follows by normal
ordering the exponential, expanding the two finite ladder matrix elements
on |n>, and summing
L_n(x)=sum_(k=0)^n binom(n,k)(-x)^k/k!. Thus the vacuum-minus-n reference
effect is 2 exp(-g^2v_p/2)[1-L_n(g^2d_pr^2)]. Its leading term is
2n g^2 d_pr^2. Equation (5) is an exact harmonic-reference identity;
the actual rotor error remains O_T(g^4), so its fourth-order coefficient
is not automatically an actual-rotor prediction to that order.

## 3. A phase-sensitive finite-state example

Fix a mode with d_pr !=0 and let

    psi_theta=sqrt(1-p)|0>+exp(i theta)sqrt(p)|2_r>, 0<p<1.

Every theta has the same occupation 2p and the same harmonic excitation
energy 2p omega_r. Yet

    N_p=2p d_pr^2,
    A_p=d_pr^2 sqrt(2p(1-p)) exp(i theta-2i omega_r s),

and the leading normalized deficit is

    D_p/(2g^2 d_pr^2)
          =2p+sqrt(2p(1-p))cos(theta-2omega_r s)+O_T(g^2). (6)

At p=0.1 and s=0, theta=0 and theta=pi give respectively
0.6242640687... and -0.2242640687..., despite equal excitation energy and
number. These are legal finite-Hermite preparations, with even parity,
not an infinite squeezed-state assumption. The negative sign is a
coincidence excess relative to the vacuum preparation, not a negative
probability. Equation (6) resolves an order-g^2 difference with an
order-g^4 remainder in the actual supplied rotor model.

Thus the particular instantaneous mean cannot be an affine nonzero-gain
function only of harmonic photon number or energy across this full
prepared-state class. It contains phase-sensitive second moments. This
counterexample is about the stated statistic and preparations, not a
theorem excluding calibrated number inference from larger record sets.

## 4. Summing probes gives a controlled energy response after averaging

Choose one readout pair for each of the 3V oriented plaquettes, each
plaquette counted once. These probes may share labels; no independence
of their random counts is used. Only linearity of the sum of their
expectations enters. The exact curl identity yields

    sum_p d_pr d_pt = sqrt(lambda_r) delta_rt/2.

Consequently (3) summed over p becomes

    sum_p [<X_p(s)^2>_psi-v_p]
       =sum_r sqrt(lambda_r)
          [n_r+Re(eta_r exp(-2i omega_r s))],                (7)
    n_r=<a_r* a_r>, eta_r=<a_r^2>.

The vacuum-subtracted harmonic excitation energy in hbar=1 units is
E_exc=sum_r omega_r n_r. For any fixed T>0, (4) and (7) give

    (1/(g^2 T)) int_0^T sum_p D_p(g,s) ds
       =tau_* E_exc
        +sum_r sqrt(lambda_r) Re[eta_r exp(-i omega_r T)]
                                         sin(omega_r T)/(omega_r T)
        +O_T(g^2).                                         (8)

The absolute size of the explicit oscillatory term is at most
(tau_*/T)sum_r |eta_r|. A number-sector state has every eta_r=0, so its
summed leading response already equals tau_*E_exc at every time.
For general finite-Hermite states the ordered limit, first g->0 at fixed
T and then T->infinity, equals tau_*E_exc. We do not exchange the limits:
the packet error C_T is not controlled uniformly as T grows.

This supplies a possible calibrated mean-energy estimator using original
record statistics, with the exact preparation and limiting premises.
Restoring physical energy units would give tau_*E_phys/hbar, subject to
that physical identification. It does not select hbar, a, c or the state.
It also does not turn the random events into absorbed photons, establish
an energy-conserving detector transition, or determine higher counting
correlations. The statistics of a sum of overlapping probe counts need
their own covariance bound before a repetition cost is assigned.

At a fixed finite graph, the separately proved bounded global dissipator
comparison changes any boundary expectation by at most O(kappa G T),
G=sum_m||B_m||^2. Therefore (4) and (8) may also compare the full
unrestricted background process with the Hamiltonian reference if
kappa G T=o(g^2), with fixed T first. This bound is attributed to the
unrestricted-count independent PRE. It permits background events; it
does not make a fixed positive physical rate disappear from the model.
At positive lag each fixed row admits the usual continuity limit; no
new quantitative arbitrary-state lag rate is claimed here.

## 5. Operational comparison with photon detection

Glauber's ideal absorption detector uses the positive-frequency field
to remove a quantum; its one-count and two-count effects have the
normally ordered forms E^-E^+ and E^-E^-E^+E^+. These statements have
the source's ideal point-detector and absorption-response assumptions.
See the primary lecture, RMP78,1267 (2006), equations 10–16 and 18–20,
DOI https://doi.org/10.1103/RevModPhys.78.1267.

For a single harmonic mode this ideal first intensity is proportional
to <n>; the two-count intensity is proportional to <n(n-1)>. Its vacuum
has no ideal absorption and an ideal one-photon state cannot yield two
absorptions. These are algebraic consequences of the specified detector
coupling. The original formation first effect 5I and selected pair effect
(1) are different effects. The immediate pair statistic has the bound
(2), and the example (6) distinguishes equal-number preparations. A
literal identification of these particular raw records with those
ideal absorption counts therefore fails these tests. A different
physical detector preparation or coarse-grained history remains open.

An empirical benchmark is Brouri et al., *Photon antibunching in the
fluorescence of individual color centers in diamond*, quant-ph/0007032v1,
https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0007032v1. Their two avalanche detectors,
beam splitter and emitting defect are not the local j,l arrangement
above. The reported zero-delay signal correlation approaches zero only
after a separately motivated background correction. Their equations
(1)–(2) distinguish raw normalized coincidences C_N from signal g^(2).
For the reported signal fraction rho=0.34, a corrected g^(2)(0)=0
corresponds to raw C_N(0)=1-rho^2=0.8844, which lies INSIDE (2).

Therefore comparing the floor 0.84 directly to that background-corrected
plot would be invalid. Neither equality of raw ranges nor a difference
from an ideal detector proves agreement or disagreement with that
experiment without deriving the source, two detection arms, backgrounds,
timing response and measurement mapping. No experimental data points
were fitted or digitized here. The paper is used to identify the actual
operational test and to prevent a false apples-to-apples comparison.

